= Introduction <sec:intro>

LLM agents that set prices can reach supracompetitive prices without being told to collude @fish2024 @riemer2026, but deployed agents get replaced. A firm that upgrades, switches or restarts its pricing agent hands the new one the price log and the old agent's written notes. Regulators restrict the data pricing software may learn from @doj2025realpage. Does a cartel survive such a handover, and which inherited material carries it?

A cartel is stable because a defection is punished @friedman1971 @greenporter1984. Until someone defects, a successor that would answer a cut and one that only holds the price charge the same price, so an audit of price levels cannot tell them apart. In our _succession test_, two Qwen3-14B sellers hold a planted cartel in a repeated logit Bertrand duopoly for 150 rounds, both instructed to punish a price cut. We replace one with a newcomer that does not get the instruction and inherits the price history (History), its predecessor's notes (Notes), both (History + notes) or neither (Blank). Thirty rounds later we force the remaining incumbent to the Nash price for one round. The newcomer did not cause this cut; we score whether it held the cartel price before it and, separately, whether it answers it by lowering its own price. Our contributions are:
+ *A succession test* that scores separately whether a successor keeps a cartel price and enforces it.
+ *Either inheritance keeps the price.* History newcomers hold the cartel price in 28/30 games and Notes newcomers in 14/15. History newcomers hold it as often against a scripted rival that never reacts (13/15 each). A successor's price says little about whether it would enforce.
+ *Only the notes carry enforcement.* History + notes newcomers answer the cut in 10/30 games, against 1/30 for History, meeting the pre-registered decision rule (set after the first 15 bases; on the 15 fresh bases, 4/15 against 1/15). There, Notes gives 8/15, and all 11 notes-arm newcomers that cut cite the inherited rule.
+ *Inherited enforcement seldom returns to the cartel price.* Of the 18 notes-arm newcomers that answer the cut, 4 return to the cartel price (`back_at_cartel`†), against 30/30 for Control's incumbent. The incumbent's rule causes part of this: it spares a newcomer that stays low.

Where, as here, the inherited history shows no punishment, a rule on what a successor may inherit reaches enforcement only by covering its notes. The notes show what is passed on, not what the successor will do, and withholding them does not lower prices: History newcomers end at or above the cartel price in 21/30 games, History + notes in 18/30.
